>
  <Header />

  <section class="bg-gopro-charcoal text-white py-16 border-b-4 border-gopro-orange">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <nav class="text-sm mb-6 font-sans">
        <a href="/" class="text-gray-400 hover:text-gopro-orange transition-colors">Home</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-gray-500">/</span>
        <a href="/guides" class="text-gray-400 hover:text-gopro-orange transition-colors">Guides</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-gray-500">/</span>
        <span class="text-white">Roof Black Streaks</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-4 tracking-tight">Roof Black Streaks in Osceola County: Soft Wash Cleaning Guide</h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 max-w-3xl font-sans">
        What causes black streaks on Central Florida roofs and how soft washing removes them safely without damaging shingles or tile.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-padding">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
        <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">
          <p class="text-xl text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6 font-sans">
            Black streaks on roofs in <a href="/areas/kissimmee" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Kissimmee</a>, <a href="/areas/st-cloud" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">St. Cloud</a>, and Osceola County are caused by Gloeocapsa magma—a type of algae that thrives in Florida's heat and humidity. The streaks don't just look bad; they hold moisture and shorten roof life. <a href="/services/power-washing" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Soft washing</a> (low-pressure cleaning with algae-killing solution) removes the streaks safely without damaging shingles, tile, or metal roofs.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gopro-orange/10 border-l-4 border-gopro-orange p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-gray-700 font-sans mb-0">
              <strong>Important:</strong> We are NOT a roofing company. We provide soft wash cleaning to remove algae and black streaks from roofs. We don't repair, replace, or inspect roofs. If your roof has leaks, missing shingles, or structural damage, hire a licensed roofer. This guide covers cleaning only.
            </p>
          </div>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">What Causes Black Streaks on Roofs?</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            The black streaks on Central Florida roofs are Gloeocapsa magma—a blue-green algae that feeds on the limestone filler in asphalt shingles. It spreads via airborne spores and grows fastest on north-facing roof sections (less direct sun, more shade and moisture).
          </p>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Tile roofs (concrete or clay) also develop black streaks, but it's usually a mix of algae, mold, and mildew rather than pure Gloeocapsa. Metal roofs get streaks too, mostly from airborne dirt and algae spores that settle in textured finishes.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Why Black Streaks Are a Problem</h2>
          
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">1. Shortens Roof Life</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Algae holds moisture against the shingles, which accelerates breakdown. On asphalt shingles, this causes granule loss and cracking. On tile, it traps moisture that seeps into the underlayment. A streaky roof in Osceola County can lose 2-5 years of life compared to a clean roof.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">2. Increases Cooling Costs</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Black algae absorbs heat. A dark, streaky roof is 10-15°F hotter than a clean roof. That extra heat transfers into your attic and raises AC costs in Florida's summer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">3. Hurts Curb Appeal & Home Value</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Black streaks make a 5-year-old roof look 20 years old. Buyers think "roof replacement" and low-ball offers. Cleaning the roof before listing can add $3,000-$8,000 to perceived value in <a href="/areas/celebration" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Celebration</a>, <a href="/areas/kissimmee" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Kissimmee</a>, and <a href="/areas/orlando" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Orlando</a> markets.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Why Soft Wash (Not Pressure Wash)?</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            High-pressure washing (2,500+ PSI) strips shingle granules, cracks tile, and forces water under shingles and flashing—causing leaks. Roof manufacturers and the Asphalt Roofing Manufacturers Association (ARMA) recommend soft wash ONLY.
          </p>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Soft wash uses low pressure (under 500 PSI) + a bleach-based cleaning solution that kills algae, mold, and mildew. The chemical does the work, not the water force. We apply the solution, let it sit 10-15 minutes, and rinse with low pressure. The algae dies and washes away without roof damage.
          </p>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Read more: <a href="/guides/soft-wash-vs-pressure-wash-kissimmee" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Soft Wash vs Pressure Wash for Kissimmee Stucco & Roofs</a>.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Soft Wash Roof Cleaning Cost</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Roof soft washing in Osceola County costs $0.20-$0.50 per square foot, depending on roof size, pitch, and accessibility.
          </p>
          <ul class="list-none space-y-2 mb-6 text-gray-700 font-sans">
            <li><strong>Small house (1,000-1,500 sq ft roof):</strong> $200-$450</li>
            <li><strong>Medium house (1,500-2,500 sq ft roof):</strong> $400-$800</li>
            <li><strong>Large house (2,500-3,500 sq ft roof):</strong> $700-$1,400</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Price depends on roof pitch (steep roofs take longer), number of stories (two-story = more setup time), and how dirty the roof is. Heavy algae or lichen requires stronger solution and longer dwell time.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">The Soft Wash Process</h2>
          
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">1. Roof Inspection</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Before cleaning, we inspect for loose shingles, cracked tiles, or damaged flashing. If we find major issues, we recommend hiring a roofer before cleaning. Soft wash is safe for structurally sound roofs, but it won't fix existing damage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">2. Protect Landscaping</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            We pre-wet plants, shrubs, and grass around the house. The cleaning solution is diluted bleach, which is safe for landscaping when rinsed properly, but we take precautions to avoid overexposure.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">3. Apply Cleaning Solution</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            We spray a mix of sodium hypochlorite (bleach), water, and surfactant onto the roof using a low-pressure pump sprayer. Start at the bottom and work up to avoid streaking. The solution is visible as it coats the roof—looks foamy or sudsy.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">4. Dwell Time (10-15 Minutes)</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Let the solution sit. This is when the chemical kills the algae. If we rinse too soon, the algae doesn't die and comes back within months. Proper dwell time = longer-lasting clean.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">5. Low-Pressure Rinse</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Rinse with low pressure (under 500 PSI) from top to bottom. The dead algae washes off. Heavy rain over the next 2-3 weeks continues to rinse residual stains, so the roof looks even better after a few storms.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">6. Final Landscaping Rinse</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Rinse plants, grass, and shrubs again to wash off any runoff solution. Takes 5-10 minutes. Properly diluted solution + rinsing = no plant damage.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">How Long Does a Clean Roof Stay Clean?</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Soft washing kills algae at the root, so roofs stay clean 2-4 years in Central Florida (vs. 6-12 months with pressure washing, which only removes surface stains). North-facing roof sections (more shade, less sun) may show streaks sooner than south-facing sections.
          </p>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            To extend clean time, some homeowners add zinc or copper strips to the roof ridge. Rainwater washes trace metals down the roof, which inhibits algae growth. Strips cost $50-$150 and can add 1-2 years to the clean period.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">What About Tile Roofs?</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Tile roofs (concrete or clay) are common in <a href="/areas/kissimmee" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Kissimmee</a> and Central Florida. They develop black streaks from algae, mold, and dirt. Soft wash works great on tile—kills organic growth without cracking or dislodging tiles.
          </p>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            <strong>Important:</strong> Never walk on tile roofs. Tiles crack under foot traffic, especially older clay tiles. We clean from ladders and use extension wands to reach the ridge without stepping on tiles.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">What About Metal Roofs?</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Metal roofs (standing seam, corrugated) get black streaks from airborne dirt and algae. Soft wash removes them safely. Metal handles bleach fine (won't corrode at the dilution we use), but we rinse thoroughly to avoid residue buildup.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">DIY vs. Hiring a Pro</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            DIY roof soft washing costs $50-$150 in materials (pump sprayer, bleach, surfactant, garden hose). Sounds cheap, but:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 font-sans">
            <li><strong>Ladder safety:</strong> Roof work is the #1 cause of homeowner falls and injuries. We use harnesses, proper ladders, and two-person teams.</li>
            <li><strong>Wrong dilution:</strong> Too weak = algae doesn't die. Too strong = plant damage or roof discoloration.</li>
            <li><strong>Incomplete rinse:</strong> Bleach residue left on roof can bleach shingles or damage underlayment over time.</li>
            <li><strong>Missed spots:</strong> DIYers often miss back slopes or areas behind chimneys, so streaks reappear fast.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            For single-story homes with easy roof access, DIY might work. For two-story, steep, or tile roofs, the $300-$800 cost is worth avoiding the fall risk and ensuring complete algae removal.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Black streaks on Osceola County roofs are Gloeocapsa magma algae. Soft wash (low pressure + bleach solution) removes them safely and keeps roofs clean 2-4 years. Costs $200-$1,400 depending on roof size. Never use high-pressure washing on roofs—it strips granules and causes leaks. If your roof has structural damage, hire a licensed roofer before cleaning.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-lg p-8 my-8">
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide">Need Roof Soft Washing in Osceola County?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 font-sans mb-6">
              Safe algae removal for shingle, tile, and metal roofs. No damage, long-lasting clean. Free estimates.
            </p>
            <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4">
              <a href="/estimate" class="btn-primary">
                Get Free Estimate
              </a>
              <a href="tel:4072446873" class="btn-secondary">
                Call (407) 244-6873
              </a>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="mt-12 pt-8 border-t border-gray-200">
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 font-sans">
              <a href="/guides" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">← Back to All Guides</a>
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

